{
  "step1_summary": "- Chief complaint: Acute, very strong left-sided headache (started this morning, sudden onset, severity ~6/10).\n- Symptoms: Sudden onset ~4 hours ago; unilateral (left side, around/behind the eye); associated blurred vision and visual phenomena described as 'zigzag lines' (visual aura); dizziness; photophobia/sensitivity to screens; nausea with two episodes of vomiting; worse on movement; paracetamol (taken ~1 hour after onset) ineffective. No prior similar headaches.\n- Past medical history: Kidney stones. No other chronic illnesses reported.\n- Medications / allergies: No regular medications. No known drug allergies.\n- Family / social history: Mother has migraines; mother also has hypothyroidism. No other family history elicited. Pregnancy felt unlikely by patient.\n- Vital signs / exam findings: No vitals recorded. Clinician noted no reported limb weakness, normal walking/gait, no other focal neurological symptoms reported in the consultation.\n- Labs / imaging: None mentioned.\n\nAdditional contextual notes: Patient is worried about serious causes (brain tumour, stroke). First-time headache of this pattern. GP provisionally suggested migraine and recommended analgesia/antiemetic and follow-up neurological assessment if not improving.",
  "step2_vindicate": {
    "V": [
      "Subarachnoid hemorrhage (SAH): Sudden-onset headache with vomiting can indicate SAH; must be considered for sudden severe headache even if not maximal intensity.",
      "Posterior circulation ischemia/stroke: Visual disturbance, dizziness and headache can reflect posterior circulation events, though strokes usually have focal deficits."
    ],
    "I": [
      "Meningitis/Encephalitis: Headache, vomiting, photophobia can be seen in CNS infection; absence of fever/neck stiffness argues against but early infection possible."
    ],
    "N": [
      "Intracranial mass (brain tumour): Can cause headache and visual changes with vomiting from raised intracranial pressure; usually more subacute/progressive rather than sudden."
    ],
    "D": [
      "Idiopathic intracranial hypertension (pseudotumor cerebri): Headache, visual disturbance and vomiting; more common in young obese women and usually subacute with visual field changes rather than transient zigzags."
    ],
    "I2": [
      "Medication or substance-related headache (e.g., caffeine withdrawal, analgesic rebound): Patient denies regular meds; sudden onset and visual aura make this less likely but still a consideration if substance changes occurred."
    ],
    "C": [
      "Arteriovenous malformation (AVM) or vascular malformation: Can present with hemorrhage causing sudden headache and vomiting; no trauma reported but congenital vascular lesions are possible causes."
    ],
    "A": [
      "Migraine with aura (neurovascular/vascular mechanism) — fits autoimmune/allergic bucket loosely as neurovascular phenomenon: Visual aura, unilateral headache, nausea/vomiting consistent with migraine pathophysiology."
    ],
    "T": [
      "Occult head trauma: Could cause headache and visual symptoms if recent head injury occurred, but patient did not report trauma."
    ],
    "E": [
      "Metabolic causes (e.g., hypoglycemia, thyroid-related headache): No evidence of metabolic derangement noted; family history of hypothyroidism but no personal symptoms or meds."
    ]
  },
